e to sample loss inherent to  processing and generally low sample  conversion rates of available library  preparation chemistries  related to some of these limitations  fornication-based library preparation  workflows have trouble fitting nicely  into the evolving paradigm of clinical  sequencing needs that i described  earlier  dna library preparation chemistries  based on enzymatic fragmentation are an  attractive alternative that alleviate  the monetary barrier to entry and  bottleneck of sequential sample  processing and automatability  however existing enzymatic  fragmentation-based chemistries are not  without their own limitations  particularly undesirable are increased  bias relative to fonication and the  introduction of sequencing artifacts  that result from use of previous or  existing products  any new enzymatic fragmentation based  product needs to successfully pair the  sequence accuracy and low bias of  sonication-based library prep with the  scalability and input compatibility of  enzymatic fragmentation based products  we saw an opportunity to leverage our  domain expertise and industry experience  to tackle this problem  we have specific performance goals in  mind  to lower bias  minimize artifacts generated during  library preparation deliver high  conversion efficiency as well as  maintain flexibility with respect to  input requirements for either high  quality dna or dna extracted from fixed  tissues  through the remainder of this section  i'll show you some of the data we  generated that supports the assertion  that we've done a pretty good job  accomplishing these goals  we ultimately developed the watchmaker  dna library prep kit with fragmentation  and the following key characteristics  and workflow details  our workflow incorporates an integrated  fragmentation and repair and a tailing  step followed by a same tube high  efficiency ligation reaction  this reaction is powered by a ligation  master mix built to improve liquid  handling and ease of automatability  pcr-free workflows can be finished in an  hour and a half of reaction time while  pcr based workflows require up to an  additional 30 minutes with an additional  b cleanup  our kit supports dna inputs from 500  nanograms to less than one nanogram with  consistent conversion rates and highly  tunable fragmentation for all input  amounts achieved just by adjusting the  time and or temperature of incubation  as i mentioned before our research and  development strategy is focused on  efficiently sampling a large reaction  space and understanding the multi-step  activities of enzymes we use  as a result we generate large data sets  that ideally report reaction optima as  well as reaction failures at extremes  our efforts optimizing for decreased gc  coverage bias is a great example of this  strategy with real utility for users  increases in uniform coverage ultimately  lead to increased sequence economy and  better data  shown here is normalized coverage data  from a series of pcr-free libraries  generated from human gdna  under different reaction conditions  modulating enzyme variants buffer  composition or the protocol  each coverage plot reports normalized  coverage on the y-axis as a function of  gc content on the x-axis  not only have we identified how to  minimize bias for the sample type we  better understand how to manipulate bias  in this reaction in either direction  from ideal  this type of comprehensive  characterization serves two purposes  one it allows us to quickly iterate and  customize product performance based on  future user needs  two it allows us to produce a base  product with desirable bias  characteristics relative to existing  products  shown here is a data set generated from  either our dna library prep kit and two  competitor kits  again we're reporting normalized  coverage data from samples generated  from high quality human gdna in this  particular experiment coverage plots  were generated from one nanogram of  input an