rge problems detecting low allele  frequency mutations  in addition groups have recently  excuse me groups recently have observed  a more complicated problem with these  types of n-localized artifacts  last year gregory adall presented an  analysis of the artifacts commonly  generated at the ends of reeds when  using enzymatic fragmentation-based kits  they suggest that with sufficient  sequence complementarity hairpins may  form at the ends of reads during the end  repair step that may be resolved into  sequenceable molecules through the  mechanism they proposed at the left of  this slide  regardless of the actual mechanisms that  drive this phenomenon this sort of  contaminating data ultimately manifests  as a problem shown at the right  when compared to libraries generated  from sonication in the blue overlay  enzymatic fragmentation based libraries  in red accumulate significantly higher  called variants shown on the y-axis as a  function of varying allele frequencies  shown on the x-axis notice how  enzymatically fragmented samples show a  much larger distribution of variants  below 10 variant allele frequency than  the sonicated samples  often noisy sequences like these at the  end of reads can automatically be  trimmed or soft-clicked during analysis  which may only create a problem for  sequence economy but every bit of  increased stringency of filtering runs  the risk of removing true positives  more concerning than the soft clip rates  are those reads that are not soft  clipped due to aligning to semi  palindromic palindromic sequences  these non-clipped reeds result in  inflated smvs and indel rates at the  ends of reeds due to the similar but not  identical sequence character of the  molecules they're derived from  therefore improvements in chemistry can  directly increase both sensitivity and  specificity of variant calling  members of our bioinformatics team  generated a similar artifact detection  tool as that used by gregory at all and  used it to visualize the frequency of  hairpin artifacts generated by our  chemistry  two competitors and compared them to  samples processed by sonication this  data is reported as a rate in the form  of artifacts per million fragments  again similar to sonication-based  library prep methods our chemistry  generates libraries with very few of  these artifacts each of which represents  a potential  potential false variant call  i've spoken about the importance of  decreasing bias and minimizing artifacts  in the context of variant calls as a  function of  improved chemistry but a similarly  important and straightforward path to  improve data quality is to capture a  higher percent of the input to begin  with  to that end we executed a head-to-head  comparison measuring the library yield  and found that our kit generates overall  higher yield than some competitors when  preparing libraries from either high  quality dna inputs or from formal and  compromised reference dna  the graph shown at the left reports the  concentration of libraries in nanomolar  on the y-axis  each sample was prepared in triplicate  from 25 nanograms of either high quality  gdna  moderately formally uncompromised or  severely formed uncompromised dna  samples  purchased from horizon discovery  yields are shown after six cycles of pcr  the electropharygrams on the right  illustrate consistent library sizing  among variable quality samples prepared  with our kit  our chemistry offers the performance  increases i just discussed but not at  the expense of highly desirable  characteristics like insert size  tunability and compatibility with a wide  range of inputs  tunable fragmentation is a key advantage  of sonication-based library preparation  and so ideally any enzymatic solution  should perform well in this respect  unfortunately the lack of tunable and  reproducible fragmentation has been a  pain point for many users of enzymatic  fragmentation even going so far as to  have great difficulty making libraries  with insert sizes at or greater than 500  base pairs  shown on the left are traces of  libraries prepared only with differing  fragmentation times  within this panel we show that not only  is insert size tunable from greater than  500 base pairs to less than 200 base  pairs fragmentation is consistent  between duplicate reactions  fragmentation is also consistent across  input amounts with acceptable ranges  spanning many orders of magnitude  the panel at the right shows overlaid  traces of libraries prepared in parallel  from 500 nanograms down to 0.1 nanogram  of input dna  in fact libraries can be generated from  inputs as low as one picogram of high  quality dna  using human human gdna as input we were  able to generate libraries from 1 000  kilograms to 1 picogram without a change  in library size  the libraries generated in this  experiment are not only sized  appropriately shown on the image on the  left but the vast majority of reads  aligned to the reference without  significant adapter contamination  illustrated by the graph on the right  overall our kit delivers high  performance at vanishingly low input  masses and expands the potential  application space for this product into  single cell territory  with such low sample inputs pcr is  required to generate sequencer-ready  libraries as a result a robust  high-fidelity library amplification  module is a necessary companion to our  library preparation kit  as i mentioned before errors in  sequencing data can come from any point  in the multi-step process from a cell to  a sequencer and many groups have  expended the effort to quantify the  extent and source of errors  one important source is the fidelity of  library amplification polymerases  a report earlier this year in genome  biology set out to quantify the error  rates of library amplification products  as well as  characterize error rates of individual  instruments at different institutions  in the figure shown in the leftmost  panel they report 